#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121089 :tutorial/content "Given a sphere with radius $r=4\\:\\textrm{cm},$ how do we calculate its surface area?\n\n![](images/bd/bd7da8538c268a1dd9e17dc8f4185ba3db5847dca21d1eb408d0af2683ba64d4.png)\n\nWe can calculate the surface area $S$ of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nS = 4\\pi r^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=4\\:\\textrm{cm}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nS = 4 \\pi (4)^2\n   = 64\\pi \\:\\textrm{cm}^2.\n$$\n\nWe often express the surface area of a sphere as a multiple of $\\pi.$ We can also get a numerical approximation using a calculator. In this case, we have\n\n$$\nS \\approx 201.06 \\:\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :tutorial/id #uuid "1896920b-682d-58fe-874e-43fed82f00a0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5396 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
